Since Chaos Master's recent anouncement of a Portuguese translation brought 
up Yet Another I18N Thread, I thought I might describe what I've been up to 
in that area....

The first idea I had was nothing less than a reinvention of printf.  This 
achieves several goals:  it assures that the POSIX positional notation of, 
e.g., %5$s is available on all platforms; and it provides a means of 
encoding grammatical information into the format string, which gettext can 
substitute, rather than into function parameters, which gettext cannot 
change.  The printf replacement takes a type string which describes the 
variable parameters passed to it, a format string, and zero or more 
variable parameters.  To pull an example more or less out of thin air
(which may or may not be what's actually coded):

    pline("osi", _("%{Nxt} is welded to your %{np3}!"),
          P_o(uwep), P_s(body_part(HAND)), P_i(bimanual(uwep)? 2 : 1));

The first parameter specifies that there are three variable parameters:  an 
object, a string, and an integer.  The second is the format string, 
enclosed in _(...) because it will be substitutible by gettext.  The rest 
are the variable parameters.  The P_x functions simply return their 
arguments and can be compiled out; they exist so that the variable 
parameter can be passed to a prototype, and a script can verify that the 
P_x functions match the type string, helping to catch any type-mismatch 
bugs.

The two format specifiers both have a left brace for the type specifier.  
When that occurs, everything up to the next right brace is considered to 
describe how the string is to be formatted.  The parameter and the stuff 
between the braces are passed to a function in a language backend, which 
can be selected at runtime, and which is free to interpret these parameters 
as the translator may think proper.

The format strings shown here are interpreted as follows:

    Nxt -- N for the name of the object; x means format as with the existing
           xname function; t means prefix with "the" if that is proper; and
           finally, because the N is uppercase, convert the first character
           to uppercase.

    np3 -- n for the name, i.e. the string itself; p to make it plural; and
           because a number follows p, that number indicates a parameter
           giving the number of things (and so this formatter pluralizes
           only conditionally).  In this case it's parameter 3, which is
           P_i(bimanual(uwep)? 2 : 1), that is, how many hands is the weapon
           welded to.  By giving the number of hands, and not a flag meaning
           make it plural or not, life is made easier for languages with
           dual forms or other multiple plurals.

The usual printf-style formatters are also supported (except for floating 
point, which Nethack doesn't use and is thus more trouble than it's worth).  
When the parameter is a monster or an object, %s is equivalent to %{} and 
is expected to render the name in some default way.  For English, objects 
are rendered as for xname and monsters as for mon_nam.

An extension to the positional notation is that parameter 0 refers to the 
player.  Thus for instance messages that change with the sex of the player 
can be handled correctly even if the player is not explicitly among the 
parameters.  "You look as ugly as ever." could be translated in Spanish as 
"Pareces tan fe%0${o} como siempre.", and even though its pline invocation 
doesn't have the player among its parameters, the sex of the player will 
still be available to produce "feo" or "fea" as appropriate.

Another point concerns the wishing code.  I made this job easier by 
separating the dungeon-feature creation into a new wizard mode command, 
#dfeature, so that the wishing function deals only in objects.  This done, 
I was able to separate it into a language-dependent parser which returns a 
structure describing the wished object, and a language-independent part 
which imposes the usual limitations on wishing.  The parser is then made 
part of the language backend.

I think I have most of the language dependencies out of the main code, and 
I'm going through putting in the _(...) bracketings for gettext.  Now to 
learn how to use gettext. :-/

